# {{SHORT_TITLE}} — Writing, Figure, Code and Review Guide

This guide governs every chapter of *{{TITLE}}*. Chapter authors, revisers and reviewers
all work from it. The LaTeX project lives in `Overleaf/`; the chapter template is
`docs/chapter-template.tex`; the shared style file is `Overleaf/textbook.sty`; the
requirements the book must satisfy are in `docs/requirements.md`.

## 1. The book and its reader

* **Purpose.** {{PURPOSE}}
* **Reader.** {{READER}}
* **Voice.** A patient tutor. Plain {{SPELLING}} English, short sentences (aim for a mean
  under 25 words), active voice, second person is welcome ("you can now..."). Intuition
  first, formalism second, code third. Never write "obviously", "clearly", "trivially" or
  "it is easy to see".
* **Promise to the reader.** Every method gets: a motivation, a plain-words idea, exact
  definitions, pseudocode (where it is an algorithm), a fully worked example with a figure
  and a trace table, its properties with proofs or proof sketches, {{CODE_LANGUAGE}} code
  that runs, pitfalls, its place in {{APPLICATION_CONTEXT}}, a summary, and exercises.

## 2. Chapter structure (mandatory)

Follow `docs/chapter-template.tex` in this order:

1. `\chapter{...}\label{ch:chNN}` (label is the file prefix, e.g. `ch:ch04`).
2. `objectives` box with 4–6 outcomes.
3. *Why this matters* (½–1 page, a concrete scenario from {{APPLICATION_CONTEXT}}, roadmap).
4. *The idea in plain words* with a `keyidea` box and at least one concept figure.
5. *Problem statement and notation* (`definition` environments).
6. *The algorithm / the method*: `algorithm2e` pseudocode + line-by-line walkthrough.
7. *A worked example*: small instance, figure, trace table, numbers verified by code.
8. *Properties*: correctness, optimality, complexity (or the method's guarantees and
   limits) as `theorem`/`proposition` with proof or proof sketch; state assumptions exactly.
9. *Variants and extensions* (short; point to later chapters where relevant).
10. *Implementation notes* + code listing + at least two `pitfall` boxes in the chapter.
11. *Where this fits* (`applicationbox`, referencing the system/closing chapter and the
    course-plan appendix where the book has them).
12. `summary` box (bullets) and a *Further reading* paragraph with citations.
13. *Exercises*: 6–10, graded `\difficulty{1..3}`; conceptual, pen-and-paper and coding;
    the coding exercise the requirements assign to this topic must appear.

**Length.** {{LENGTH_RULE}} Completeness and accuracy outrank length; cuts target
repetition and padding, never required content. Introduction, toolbox, system and
evaluation chapters may deviate from the algorithm template but keep boxes, figures,
tables, summary and exercises.

## 3. Writing rules

* Define every symbol before it is used; use the macros of `textbook.sty` and the notation
  table (`Overleaf/frontmatter/notation.tex`). Do not invent competing notation.
* Define each acronym at first use in every chapter.
* One idea per paragraph. Lists for enumerations; tables for comparisons.
* Bold a term when it is defined and add an `\index{}` entry (on one source line). At least
  15 index entries per chapter (use subentries: `\index{term!aspect}`).
* Reference every figure, table, algorithm and listing from the text with `\cref`.
* Captions say what to notice ("The dashed path is longer but avoids the corridor.").
* Labels: `ch:chNN`, `sec:chNN-slug`, `fig:chNN-slug`, `tab:chNN-slug`, `alg:chNN-slug`,
  `eq:chNN-slug`, `def:chNN-slug`, `thm:chNN-slug`, `ex:chNN-slug` (examples),
  `exr:chNN-slug` (exercises), `lst:chNN-slug`. Cross-chapter references: `\cref{ch:ch09}`.
* Citations: `\cite{key}` from `Overleaf/references.bib`. Cite the original source and one
  textbook treatment. **Never fabricate a reference.** Add an entry only if you are certain
  of authors, title, venue and year; put it in `Overleaf/bib/chNN-extra.bib`. Omit page
  numbers rather than guess.
* Accuracy: pseudocode and formulas must match the canonical sources (Section 8). Check the
  edge cases the sources warn about. Numbers in worked examples must be produced or checked
  by the code.

## 4. Figures

* TikZ / pgfplots only; no bitmap or external images (the figure cache is generated from
  the TikZ sources by `tools/render_figures.py`).
* One figure per file: `Overleaf/figures/chNN/<slug>.tex` containing only the
  `tikzpicture` (no `figure` environment, no `\label`, no `\cref`). Include it with
  `\begin{figure}[tb]\centering\inputfigure{chNN/slug}\caption{...}\label{fig:chNN-slug}\end{figure}`.
  A picture that must contain a `\cref` or `\label` starts with `\tikzexternaldisable`.
* Use the shared styles of `textbook.sty` (`sbpath`, `sbnode`, `sbedge`, `sbagentA..D`,
  `sbvec`, `sbannot`, `sbbox`, `sbflow`, `sbregion`, grid helpers, colours `sbBlue`,
  `sbOrange`, `sbGreen`, `sbPurple`, `sbRed`, `sbGray`); add book-specific styles to the
  style file through the editor, not inside chapters.
* At least 4 figures per chapter: a concept figure, a worked-example figure, an
  output or comparison figure, and one more where useful.
* Generated figures: `Overleaf/code/figures/gen_chNN_<slug>.py` (fixed seeds) writes
  `Overleaf/figures/data/chNN-<slug>.dat` (whitespace-separated, header row) for
  `\addplot table[x=..., y=...]`, or a complete TikZ file. Run it and commit its output.
* Keep plots below ~1500 points; width at most `\textwidth`; text `\small` or `\footnotesize`.

## 5. Tables and pseudocode

* Tables use `booktabs`, `\small`, caption above. Typical tables: method comparison, trace
  of the worked example, parameters and their effect, metrics.
* Pseudocode uses `algorithm2e`: `\begin{algorithm}[htb]`, `\caption`, `\label{alg:...}`,
  `\KwIn`, `\KwOut`, `\Fn{\Name{args}}{...}`, `\KwRet`, `\ForEach`, `\While`, `\If`,
  `\tcp*{...}` for end-of-line comments. Declare function names with
  `\SetKwFunction{Uniq}{Name}` at the top of the chapter; **macro names must be unique
  across the book** (prefix with the chapter topic). Label lines with
  `\label{alg:chNN-name:step}` and refer to them with `line~\ref{alg:chNN-name:step}`.

## 6. Code

* {{CODE_POLICY}}
* Full implementation: `Overleaf/code/chNN_<slug>.py`, with a self-test (`assert`s) that
  runs in under 60 seconds when the file is executed. Run it; it must pass.
* In the chapter: `\begin{lstlisting}[caption={...},label={lst:...}] ... \end{lstlisting}`
  excerpts of at most 45 lines, copied verbatim from the file. Numbers or output quoted in
  the text come from actually running the code.

## 7. Compile check (required before a chapter is handed in)

```bash
cd Overleaf && ./build.sh chNN-slug      # builds build/only-chNN-slug.pdf
python3 code/chNN_slug.py                # self-test must pass
```

Build status 0; no `!` errors; no undefined references or citations that belong to *your*
chapter (references to other chapters show `??` in single-chapter builds); fix overfull
boxes wider than 15 pt when the fix is easy. Do not edit `textbook.sty`, `main.tex`, or
other chapters; list needed macro additions in your final report.
`pdftotext build/only-chNN-slug.pdf - | head -300` is the way to proofread your output.

